            "coverage": "Four color photographs JunoCam took on 29 September 2022, between 97 and 291 seconds after Juno passed 355 km above Europa, from 1,515 to 5,443 km up by their labels and 1.1 to 3.7 km per pixel. Each is a stack of strips through the camera's red, green and blue filters, every strip with its own camera from the Juno kernels; two epochs per photograph are fitted to Europa's lit limb. They cover 23 percent of the globe, around the sub-Jovian point, with the lighting they were taken in: the terminator runs down the western edge. The three bands share one display range, so the color is JunoCam's own band ratios, not a natural-color reconstruction. Surface the photographs do not reach keeps the grid."
